1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,410
200 meters to go, and
Jordan is sprinting all out.

2
00:00:03,460 --> 00:00:07,610
{1}Her legs are burning, {2}her heart
is pounding, {3}she's gasping for

3
00:00:07,660 --> 00:00:11,250
air, {4}and sweat is
pouring down her face.

4
00:00:11,300 --> 00:00:11,945
{5}So here's a question.

5
00:00:11,965 --> 00:00:17,511
How many parts of her body have to
work together, right now, just to keep

6
00:00:17,561 --> 00:00:18,361
her running?

7
00:00:18,701 --> 00:00:23,302
By the end of this video, you'll be
able to zoom from Jordan's whole body

8
00:00:23,352 --> 00:00:27,065
down to the molecules in
one muscle, and back out.

9
00:00:27,115 --> 00:00:29,818
{1}Those are the six
levels of organization.

10
00:00:29,868 --> 00:00:33,867
{2}You'll know all eleven organ systems
and what each one is doing in this

11
00:00:33,917 --> 00:00:38,585
sprint, {3}what makes something alive,
{4}and why her temperature barely

12
00:00:38,635 --> 00:00:40,385
moves while she runs.

13
00:00:40,435 --> 00:00:42,159
{5}It's the frame for the whole course.

14
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:45,990
Most students treat this
chapter like a vocabulary list.

15
00:00:46,040 --> 00:00:48,523
{1}But one idea ties it all together.

16
00:00:48,573 --> 00:00:52,897
{2}How a body part is built
decides what it can do.

17
00:00:52,947 --> 00:00:57,910
{3}That's the link between anatomy and
physiology, {4}and it's why the body

18
00:00:57,960 --> 00:01:02,246
is built in levels, each able
to do something its parts can't.

19
00:01:02,296 --> 00:01:06,000
{5}Keep that idea handy, it
comes back in every chapter.

20
00:01:09,050 --> 00:01:11,793
So what's actually the difference
between anatomy and physiology?

21
00:01:11,843 --> 00:01:14,267
Let's use Jordan's heart.

22
00:01:14,317 --> 00:01:16,920
{1}You can ask what it looks like.

23
00:01:16,970 --> 00:01:21,400
It's about the size of a fist, it's
mostly muscle, and it has four chambers

24
00:01:21,450 --> 00:01:23,907
inside. {2}That's anatomy.

25
00:01:23,957 --> 00:01:25,960
{3}Or you can ask how it works.

26
00:01:26,010 --> 00:01:30,792
How does that muscle squeeze, and what
pushes it from about 70 beats a minute

27
00:01:30,842 --> 00:01:33,608
at rest to around 180 in this sprint?

28
00:01:33,658 --> 00:01:35,570
{4}That's physiology.

29
00:01:36,070 --> 00:01:41,010
Anatomy is the study of the body's
structures: their shape, size, parts

30
00:01:41,060 --> 00:01:46,210
and position. {1}The word comes from
the Greek for a cutting up, {2}because

31
00:01:46,260 --> 00:01:51,270
for most of history, dissection,
cutting a body open, was the main way to

32
00:01:51,320 --> 00:01:52,260
see inside.

33
00:01:52,760 --> 00:01:55,323
Anatomy splits two ways.

34
00:01:55,373 --> 00:02:00,217
{1}By scale: gross anatomy is what
you can see with your eyes, like the

35
00:02:00,267 --> 00:02:04,650
heart, {2}and microscopic anatomy
needs a microscope, like the cells in

36
00:02:04,700 --> 00:02:09,857
the heart wall. {3}And by approach:
regional anatomy studies everything

37
00:02:09,907 --> 00:02:15,370
in one part of the body together,
every bone, muscle, vessel and nerve in

38
00:02:15,420 --> 00:02:20,390
the hand. {4}Systemic anatomy follows
one body system through the whole

39
00:02:20,440 --> 00:02:23,894
body, all the bones, then all the muscles.

40
00:02:23,944 --> 00:02:28,530
{5}Most courses work that way, and
we'll define a system exactly in a few

41
00:02:28,580 --> 00:02:29,380
minutes.

42
00:02:29,620 --> 00:02:34,003
Physiology is the study of
how those structures work.

43
00:02:34,053 --> 00:02:39,035
{1}Physio means nature,
logy means study of.

44
00:02:39,085 --> 00:02:43,960
{2}And we'll always describe physiology
as a mechanism, one thing causing

45
00:02:44,010 --> 00:02:50,013
the next. {3}So we won'T say her heart
speeds up because she needs oxygen,

46
00:02:50,063 --> 00:02:55,280
as if it had a plan. {4}We'll say
signals from her nerves, plus adrenaline,

47
00:02:55,330 --> 00:02:59,789
a chemical messenger carried in her
blood, make her heart beat faster and

48
00:02:59,839 --> 00:03:03,490
harder. {5}That's a chain
of causes you can follow.

49
00:03:03,990 --> 00:03:05,826
Here's the big idea in action.

50
00:03:05,876 --> 00:03:10,994
{1}You can't pull anatomy and physiology
apart, because what a structure

51
00:03:11,044 --> 00:03:13,479
can do depends on how it's built.

52
00:03:13,529 --> 00:03:16,806
{2}Take the tiniest blood
vessels, the capillaries.

53
00:03:16,856 --> 00:03:23,786
{3}Their wall is just one layer of
thin, flat cells, {4}so oxygen slips

54
00:03:23,836 --> 00:03:27,826
right through it, out of the blood
and into Jordan's leg muscle.

55
00:03:27,876 --> 00:03:30,886
{5}A thick walled vessel couldn't do that.

56
00:03:30,936 --> 00:03:32,876
Structure decides function.

57
00:03:33,376 --> 00:03:38,997
Quick check. {1}A student measures how
fast blood is flowing through Jordan's

58
00:03:39,047 --> 00:03:42,819
arteries, the vessels carrying
blood away from her heart.

59
00:03:42,869 --> 00:03:45,496
Is that anatomy or physiology?

60
00:03:49,996 --> 00:03:55,010
Physiology, because it's
about how something works.

61
00:03:55,060 --> 00:03:59,911
{1}Measuring how thick the
artery wall is would be anatomy.

62
00:03:59,961 --> 00:04:02,411
{2}Now let's see how that
structure is put together.

63
00:04:05,461 --> 00:04:06,643
So how is a body built?

64
00:04:06,693 --> 00:04:09,171
Let's dive into Jordan's thigh.

65
00:04:09,221 --> 00:04:13,507
{1}This big muscle on the front is
one of her quadriceps, the group that

66
00:04:13,557 --> 00:04:15,544
straightens her knee every stride.

67
00:04:15,594 --> 00:04:19,443
{2}It's built in levels, each made
from units of the level below, {3}and

68
00:04:19,493 --> 00:04:22,221
each level can do things
its parts can't do alone.

69
00:04:22,721 --> 00:04:25,418
At the very bottom is the chemical level.

70
00:04:25,468 --> 00:04:30,847
{1}An atom is the smallest unit of an
element, a pure substance like carbon,

71
00:04:30,897 --> 00:04:35,738
hydrogen, oxygen or nitrogen,
written C, H, O and N.

72
00:04:35,788 --> 00:04:39,898
{2}Atoms join to make a molecule,
{3}like water, two hydrogen atoms bonded

73
00:04:39,948 --> 00:04:42,338
to one oxygen. That's H2O.

74
00:04:42,388 --> 00:04:47,562
{4}Her muscle is mostly water, plus
huge molecules built mainly from those

75
00:04:47,612 --> 00:04:50,948
four elements, like the protein
strands that do the pulling.

76
00:04:51,448 --> 00:04:53,619
But a pile of molecules isn't alive.

77
00:04:53,669 --> 00:04:58,006
{1}Arrange them just right and you
get a cell, the smallest unit that can

78
00:04:58,056 --> 00:05:01,766
carry out the activities of life on
its own, and we'll pin down what those

79
00:05:01,816 --> 00:05:03,534
are later in this video.

80
00:05:03,584 --> 00:05:06,766
Cell is Latin for small room.

81
00:05:06,816 --> 00:05:11,686
{2}Her thigh's cells are long, thin
muscle cells, called muscle fibers,

82
00:05:11,736 --> 00:05:14,086
{3}and each one can shorten and pull.

83
00:05:14,136 --> 00:05:16,616
{4}A loose pile of
molecules can't do that.

84
00:05:17,116 --> 00:05:19,878
Next, the tissue level.

85
00:05:19,928 --> 00:05:23,788
{1}A tissue is a group of similar
cells, plus the material around them,

86
00:05:23,838 --> 00:05:25,898
working together on one job.

87
00:05:25,948 --> 00:05:30,511
{2}The word comes from the French for
woven, {3}and you can see why: thousands

88
00:05:30,561 --> 00:05:34,828
of fibers lie side by side, bundled
in thin wrappings, {4}and many of them

89
00:05:34,878 --> 00:05:37,578
shorten together, so
the bundle pulls as one.

90
00:05:37,628 --> 00:05:40,468
{5}Studying tissues is called histology.

91
00:05:40,968 --> 00:05:42,785
Zoom out to the organ level.

92
00:05:42,835 --> 00:05:47,825
{1}An organ is made of two or more kinds
of tissue, has a recognizable shape,

93
00:05:47,875 --> 00:05:49,718
and does a specific job.

94
00:05:49,768 --> 00:05:52,478
{2}That whole thigh muscle is an organ.

95
00:05:52,528 --> 00:05:57,078
{3}It has muscle tissue to pull, tough
connective tissue holding it together

96
00:05:57,128 --> 00:06:01,318
and tying it to bone through a tendon,
{4}blood vessels bringing oxygen,

97
00:06:01,368 --> 00:06:03,558
and nerves telling it when to pull.

98
00:06:03,608 --> 00:06:08,808
{5}Organon is Greek for tool, and this
is a tool for straightening her knee.

99
00:06:09,308 --> 00:06:14,245
An organ system is a group of organs
working together on a shared job.

100
00:06:14,295 --> 00:06:18,525
{1}Her thigh muscle is one organ of
her muscular system, {2}along with the

101
00:06:18,575 --> 00:06:22,175
other muscles attached to her
bones, more than 600 of them.

102
00:06:22,675 --> 00:06:27,845
And the organism level is Jordan
herself, {1}a whole living individual,

103
00:06:27,895 --> 00:06:30,052
all her systems working together.

104
00:06:30,102 --> 00:06:36,683
{2}So from the bottom: chemical,
cellular, tissue, organ, organ system,

105
00:06:36,733 --> 00:06:38,915
organism. {3}Six levels.

106
00:06:39,415 --> 00:06:44,629
Notice the pattern. {1}One fiber can
pull, but it can't straighten a knee.

107
00:06:44,679 --> 00:06:47,685
{2}That takes a whole
muscle anchored to bone.

108
00:06:47,735 --> 00:06:49,781
{3}And one muscle can't run a race.

109
00:06:49,831 --> 00:06:52,898
{4}That takes whole systems.

110
00:06:52,948 --> 00:06:56,095
{5}Every level gains abilities
its parts don'T have.

111
00:06:56,595 --> 00:07:01,125
Quick check. {1}Similar cells, plus
the material around them, working on

112
00:07:01,175 --> 00:07:03,115
one job. Which level?

113
00:07:07,615 --> 00:07:10,432
A tissue. {1}The trap is saying organ.

114
00:07:10,482 --> 00:07:14,805
{2}An organ needs two
or more kinds of tissue.

115
00:07:14,855 --> 00:07:18,815
{3}Now let's zoom back out and
meet all her organ systems.

116
00:07:21,865 --> 00:07:25,505
Most A&P courses group the
organs into eleven organ systems.

117
00:07:25,555 --> 00:07:30,312
{1}The easiest way to learn them is to
ask what each one is doing for Jordan

118
00:07:30,362 --> 00:07:35,351
in this sprint. {2}Learn each
one's main organs and main job.

119
00:07:35,851 --> 00:07:37,631
Let's start on the outside.

120
00:07:37,681 --> 00:07:43,381
{1}The integumentary system is her skin
plus what grows from it: hair, nails,

121
00:07:43,431 --> 00:07:47,288
sweat glands. {2}Integumentum
is Latin for covering.

122
00:07:47,338 --> 00:07:51,781
{3}It protects her, limits water loss
and helps control body temperature.

123
00:07:51,831 --> 00:07:56,045
{4}Right now it's pouring out sweat,
and as sweat evaporates, it carries

124
00:07:56,095 --> 00:07:56,895
heat away.

125
00:07:57,371 --> 00:08:00,788
The skeletal system is
her bones and joints.

126
00:08:00,838 --> 00:08:04,821
{1}It supports her and protects organs
like her brain and heart, {2}gives

127
00:08:04,871 --> 00:08:09,501
muscles something to pull on, {3}stores
calcium, and makes blood cells inside

128
00:08:09,551 --> 00:08:10,351
the bones.

129
00:08:10,711 --> 00:08:14,765
The muscular system is the
muscles attached to those bones.

130
00:08:14,815 --> 00:08:18,861
{1}They pull on the bones to move
her, {2}and they make a lot of heat as

131
00:08:18,911 --> 00:08:22,274
they work. So why doesn't
her temperature shoot up?

132
00:08:22,324 --> 00:08:23,191
We'll get there.

133
00:08:23,691 --> 00:08:25,867
So what tells those muscles when to pull?

134
00:08:25,917 --> 00:08:31,029
{1}The nervous system: brain, spinal
cord, and nerves running everywhere.

135
00:08:31,079 --> 00:08:37,403
{2}It detects changes and sends fast
electrical signals that control responses,

136
00:08:37,453 --> 00:08:41,141
{3}like the signals racing down her
nerves to her legs every stride.

137
00:08:41,641 --> 00:08:47,211
The endocrine system controls too, but
with slower, longer lasting signals.

138
00:08:47,261 --> 00:08:53,671
{1}Endo means within, and crine means
to secrete, to release: its glands

139
00:08:53,721 --> 00:08:57,191
release hormones, chemical
messengers, into the blood.

140
00:08:57,241 --> 00:09:03,111
{2}The main ones are the pituitary,
thyroid, adrenal glands, pancreas, and

141
00:09:03,161 --> 00:09:06,564
the gonads, meaning the testes or ovaries.

142
00:09:06,614 --> 00:09:11,651
{3}It runs slow processes like growth,
{4}and right now her adrenal glands

143
00:09:11,701 --> 00:09:14,921
are releasing adrenaline, which
helps push her heart rate up.

144
00:09:15,421 --> 00:09:20,704
Quick check. {1}Nervous and endocrine
systems both control her body.

145
00:09:20,754 --> 00:09:22,714
How are their signals different?

146
00:09:27,214 --> 00:09:31,944
The nervous system sends fast
electrical signals along nerves.

147
00:09:31,994 --> 00:09:36,124
{1}The endocrine system sends hormones
through the blood, and those are

148
00:09:36,174 --> 00:09:39,814
usually slower, but they last longer.

149
00:09:39,864 --> 00:09:44,473
{2}Now, all that work needs fuel and
oxygen, so let's meet the systems that

150
00:09:44,523 --> 00:09:46,438
supply her and clean up.

151
00:09:49,488 --> 00:09:53,198
The cardiovascular system is the
heart, blood vessels and blood.

152
00:09:53,248 --> 00:09:57,705
{1}Cardio means heart,
vascular means small vessel.

153
00:09:57,755 --> 00:10:02,878
{2}It pumps blood carrying oxygen,
nutrients, wastes and heat around her

154
00:10:02,928 --> 00:10:07,175
body, {3}and in a sprint, because nerve
signals and adrenaline make it beat

155
00:10:07,225 --> 00:10:11,166
faster and harder, her heart can pump
several times more blood a minute

156
00:10:11,216 --> 00:10:12,088
than at rest.

157
00:10:12,588 --> 00:10:17,626
Beside it is the lymphatic system: the
thymus, the spleen, and thin vessels

158
00:10:17,676 --> 00:10:20,338
with small filtering nodes along them.

159
00:10:20,388 --> 00:10:21,118
{1}Here's why it matters.

160
00:10:21,138 --> 00:10:26,605
Blood pressure always pushes some
fluid out through those thin capillary

161
00:10:26,655 --> 00:10:30,805
walls into the tissues, {2}and lymphatic
vessels bring it back to the blood.

162
00:10:30,855 --> 00:10:34,468
{3}It also houses many immune
cells, which fight infection.

163
00:10:34,928 --> 00:10:40,165
The respiratory system is the nose,
the trachea or windpipe, and the lungs.

164
00:10:40,215 --> 00:10:44,618
{1}Re means again, spire means breathe.

165
00:10:44,668 --> 00:10:48,918
{2}It brings oxygen into her
blood and removes carbon dioxide.

166
00:10:48,968 --> 00:10:53,778
{3}Her muscles are using oxygen and
making carbon dioxide much faster now,

167
00:10:53,828 --> 00:10:56,898
and signals from her brain
speed up her breathing to match.

168
00:10:56,948 --> 00:11:00,548
{4}How her brain sets that
pace gets its own video later.

169
00:11:01,048 --> 00:11:07,962
The digestive system is the mouth,
esophagus, stomach, intestines, liver,

170
00:11:08,012 --> 00:11:10,911
gallbladder, pancreas and appendix.

171
00:11:10,961 --> 00:11:16,238
{1}It breaks food down, absorbs nutrients,
and gets rid of the rest as feces.

172
00:11:16,288 --> 00:11:18,628
{2}Her muscles' fuel came in this way.

173
00:11:19,128 --> 00:11:24,278
The urinary system is the kidneys,
two ureters, the urinary bladder and

174
00:11:24,328 --> 00:11:29,818
the urethra. {1}It filters blood,
removes wastes as urine, and controls

175
00:11:29,868 --> 00:11:35,238
water balance. {2}And students
mix up this pair all the time.

176
00:11:35,288 --> 00:11:40,258
{3}Ureters are two tubes, one from
each kidney down to the bladder.

177
00:11:40,308 --> 00:11:44,738
{4}The urethra is one tube from
the bladder out of the body.

178
00:11:44,788 --> 00:11:47,948
Two ureters, one urethra.

179
00:11:48,448 --> 00:11:55,013
Last, the reproductive system: testes
in males; ovaries, uterine tubes,

180
00:11:55,063 --> 00:11:57,817
uterus and vagina in females.

181
00:11:57,867 --> 00:12:03,617
{1}It makes sex cells, and the female
system can support a developing baby.

182
00:12:03,667 --> 00:12:07,017
{2}It's the one system Jordan
doesn't need to stay alive.

183
00:12:07,067 --> 00:12:10,120
{3}It keeps the species
going, not the individual.

184
00:12:10,620 --> 00:12:13,830
Some organs belong to two systems.

185
00:12:13,880 --> 00:12:18,470
{1}The pancreas sends digestive juices
into the intestine, so it's digestive,

186
00:12:18,520 --> 00:12:21,650
{2}and hormones into the
blood, so it's endocrine.

187
00:12:21,700 --> 00:12:27,100
{3}The gonads make sex cells and
hormones: reproductive and endocrine.

188
00:12:30,150 --> 00:12:34,247
Every system depends on blood getting
around, so let's look at the three

189
00:12:34,297 --> 00:12:39,340
kinds of vessels. {1}Here's the
rule that beats a classic exam trap.

190
00:12:39,390 --> 00:12:45,300
{2}Vessels are named by the direction
of flow relative to the heart, not

191
00:12:45,350 --> 00:12:47,550
by how much oxygen the blood carries.

192
00:12:48,050 --> 00:12:54,413
An artery carries blood away from the
heart, {1}with thick, muscular walls,

193
00:12:54,463 --> 00:12:58,533
because the heart pushes blood
into it under high pressure.

194
00:12:58,583 --> 00:13:03,720
{2}A vein carries blood back toward
the heart, {3}with thinner walls and

195
00:13:03,770 --> 00:13:07,733
lower pressure, because by the time
blood gets through the capillaries,

196
00:13:07,783 --> 00:13:10,333
most of the heart's push is used up.

197
00:13:10,383 --> 00:13:16,373
{4}Capillaries, one cell thick, link
the smallest arteries to the smallest

198
00:13:16,423 --> 00:13:19,343
veins. That's where exchange happens.

199
00:13:19,843 --> 00:13:23,771
Arteries away, veins toward,
capillaries exchange.

200
00:13:23,821 --> 00:13:30,722
{1}Now the trap. Most arteries carry
oxygen rich blood, but not all.

201
00:13:30,772 --> 00:13:37,082
{2}The pulmonary arteries, pulmo
meaning lung, run from her heart to her

202
00:13:37,132 --> 00:13:42,610
lungs, and they carry blood that's
low in oxygen, {3}because it just came

203
00:13:42,660 --> 00:13:45,746
back from her body and
is going to pick up more.

204
00:13:45,796 --> 00:13:49,895
{4}They're still arteries: they
carry blood away from the heart.

205
00:13:49,945 --> 00:13:53,062
{5}So her blood keeps every cell supplied.

206
00:13:53,112 --> 00:13:56,603
But what makes those cells
alive in the first place?

207
00:13:59,653 --> 00:14:00,246
Here's a strange question.

208
00:14:00,266 --> 00:14:05,303
A rock and one of Jordan's
cells are both made of atoms.

209
00:14:05,353 --> 00:14:07,693
Why is only one alive?

210
00:14:07,743 --> 00:14:10,893
{1}Because living things
share seven characteristics.

211
00:14:11,393 --> 00:14:14,887
First, organization, the
six levels we just saw.

212
00:14:14,937 --> 00:14:20,153
{1}Then metabolism, from meta, meaning
change, which is all the chemical

213
00:14:20,203 --> 00:14:24,543
changes in her body. Some build big
molecules and use energy, and others

214
00:14:24,593 --> 00:14:26,203
break them down and release it.

215
00:14:26,253 --> 00:14:31,503
{2}Responsiveness: sensing a change
and reacting, like yanking her foot

216
00:14:31,553 --> 00:14:34,093
off a sharp rock before
she's thought about it.

217
00:14:34,143 --> 00:14:37,553
{3}And movement, of her body
and of things inside it.

218
00:14:38,020 --> 00:14:42,375
There's growth, from more cells,
bigger cells, or more material between

219
00:14:42,425 --> 00:14:47,897
them. {1}Development, the changes over
a lifetime, like cells specializing

220
00:14:47,947 --> 00:14:53,103
into muscle fibers. {2}And reproduction,
making new cells for growth and

221
00:14:53,153 --> 00:14:55,533
repair, and new organisms.

222
00:14:55,583 --> 00:14:57,153
{3}A rock does none of this.

223
00:14:57,653 --> 00:15:02,056
To stay alive, her cells also
need things from outside.

224
00:15:02,106 --> 00:15:04,966
{1}Oxygen, to release most
of the energy stored in food.

225
00:15:05,016 --> 00:15:09,836
Without it, energy falls within minutes,
and brain cells fail first, because

226
00:15:09,886 --> 00:15:13,336
the brain uses a lot of
energy and stores almost none.

227
00:15:13,386 --> 00:15:18,576
{2}Nutrients, the substances from food
she uses for energy, building material

228
00:15:18,626 --> 00:15:21,936
and chemical control, including
minerals and vitamins.

229
00:15:21,986 --> 00:15:26,742
{3}And water, because most of her mass
is water, and nearly every chemical

230
00:15:26,792 --> 00:15:29,026
change in her cells
happens dissolved in it.

231
00:15:29,526 --> 00:15:33,669
She also needs a narrow temperature
range, because chemical changes speed

232
00:15:33,719 --> 00:15:38,076
up when it's warmer and slow when it's
colder, {1}and too far either way,

233
00:15:38,126 --> 00:15:40,396
big molecules lose their working shape.

234
00:15:40,446 --> 00:15:42,706
{2}And suitable air pressure.

235
00:15:42,756 --> 00:15:46,656
Her breathing muscles expand her
chest, which lowers the pressure inside

236
00:15:46,706 --> 00:15:51,429
her lungs, so air flows in from the
higher pressure outside, and that pressure

237
00:15:51,479 --> 00:15:54,448
also helps push oxygen into her blood.

238
00:15:54,498 --> 00:15:58,073
{3}High on a mountain, with lower
pressure, less oxygen gets in with each

239
00:15:58,123 --> 00:15:58,923
breath.

240
00:16:01,636 --> 00:16:05,166
That temperature need brings us
back to the promise from the start.

241
00:16:05,216 --> 00:16:08,576
{1}Take Jordan's temperature
before the race and right after.

242
00:16:08,626 --> 00:16:12,341
{2}It's up a little, and her
body is already pushing it back.

243
00:16:12,391 --> 00:16:17,715
{3}It stays near 37 °C, about 98.6 °F,
though plenty of healthy people run

244
00:16:17,765 --> 00:16:22,439
a little lower, inside a normal range,
the band of values found in healthy

245
00:16:22,489 --> 00:16:27,039
people. {4}So do the water,
sodium and oxygen in her blood.

246
00:16:27,539 --> 00:16:28,673
So how does it do that?

247
00:16:28,723 --> 00:16:33,209
{1}Her body detects a change, which
triggers a response that pushes the

248
00:16:33,259 --> 00:16:37,569
value back. {2}As her muscles heat her
up, her sweat glands pour out sweat,

249
00:16:37,619 --> 00:16:40,449
{3}and as it evaporates,
it carries heat away.

250
00:16:40,499 --> 00:16:45,356
{4}In the cold it's reversed: cold
sensed by her skin and brain triggers

251
00:16:45,406 --> 00:16:49,249
shivering, small fast muscle
contractions that make heat.

252
00:16:49,299 --> 00:16:53,836
{5}When this fails and a value drifts
out of range, that's often what illness

253
00:16:53,886 --> 00:16:58,657
is. The full mechanism, the feedback
loop, comes later in the Foundations

254
00:16:58,707 --> 00:16:59,739
part of this course.

255
00:17:00,239 --> 00:17:04,413
Last big idea: no organ
system works alone.

256
00:17:04,463 --> 00:17:08,710
{1}Her thigh muscle needs, right now,
{2}oxygen brought in by the respiratory

257
00:17:08,760 --> 00:17:13,109
system, {3}delivered by the cardiovascular
system, {4}fuel absorbed by the

258
00:17:13,159 --> 00:17:17,560
digestive system, {5}and wastes
carried off in the blood, to be removed

259
00:17:17,610 --> 00:17:19,543
by her lungs and urinary system.

260
00:17:20,043 --> 00:17:21,540
Watch what happens when one fails.

261
00:17:21,590 --> 00:17:26,573
If the kidneys stop working, they stop
removing water and wastes from the

262
00:17:26,623 --> 00:17:31,726
blood. {1}Water builds up in the blood
and around cells, so ankles swell,

263
00:17:31,776 --> 00:17:36,353
as gravity pulls the extra fluid down,
and fluid can collect in the lungs.

264
00:17:36,403 --> 00:17:40,653
{2}The lungs then move less oxygen
into the blood, {3}and the extra fluid

265
00:17:40,703 --> 00:17:44,453
and higher blood pressure
make the heart work harder.

266
00:17:44,503 --> 00:17:47,780
{4}One failing organ,
three systems in trouble.

267
00:17:47,830 --> 00:17:52,083
{5}So for every system, ask what it
depends on, and what depends on it.

268
00:17:55,133 --> 00:17:56,263
Let's put it all in one picture.

269
00:17:56,313 --> 00:18:01,883
{1}Anatomy is structure, physiology
is how it works, and structure decides

270
00:18:01,933 --> 00:18:07,883
function. {2}The body is built in six
levels: chemical, cellular, tissue,

271
00:18:07,933 --> 00:18:15,099
organ, organ system, organism, each
one gaining abilities its parts don't

272
00:18:15,149 --> 00:18:20,416
have. {3}Eleven organ systems, each
with its own job, all depending on each

273
00:18:20,466 --> 00:18:25,643
other. {4}Arteries away, veins
toward, capillaries exchange.

274
00:18:25,693 --> 00:18:29,849
{5}And a living body stays alive by
holding conditions like temperature

275
00:18:29,899 --> 00:18:32,133
inside a normal range.

276
00:18:35,183 --> 00:18:36,893
Okay, your turn. Problem one.

277
00:18:36,943 --> 00:18:44,306
{1}Put these in order from smallest
to largest: organ, cell, organ system,

278
00:18:44,356 --> 00:18:46,423
molecule, tissue.

279
00:18:52,923 --> 00:19:00,433
Molecule, the chemical level, then cell,
tissue, organ, organ system, {1}and

280
00:19:00,483 --> 00:19:06,153
the organism on top. {2}The trap
is swapping tissue and organ.

281
00:19:06,203 --> 00:19:10,483
{3}An organ is built from
tissues, so it comes after them.

282
00:19:10,983 --> 00:19:16,203
Problem two. {1}The pulmonary arteries
carry blood from the heart to the

283
00:19:16,253 --> 00:19:19,083
lungs, and that blood is low in oxygen.

284
00:19:19,133 --> 00:19:22,563
{2}A student says that means they
should really be called veins.

285
00:19:22,613 --> 00:19:24,133
Right or wrong?

286
00:19:29,633 --> 00:19:33,223
Wrong. {1}Vessels are named
by direction, not oxygen.

287
00:19:33,273 --> 00:19:37,843
{2}These carry blood away from
the heart, so they're arteries.

288
00:19:37,893 --> 00:19:41,153
{3}The trap is thinking
artery means oxygen rich.

289
00:19:41,203 --> 00:19:43,713
Usually true, but not the definition.

290
00:19:44,213 --> 00:19:49,302
Problem three. {1}A patient's kidney
is fine, but a stone is stuck in the

291
00:19:49,352 --> 00:19:52,305
tube that carries urine from
that kidney to the bladder.

292
00:19:52,355 --> 00:19:54,208
{2}Which tube is blocked?

293
00:19:59,708 --> 00:20:03,818
It's a ureter. {1}There are two of
them, one for each kidney, running down

294
00:20:03,868 --> 00:20:08,597
to the bladder. {2}The trap is the
urethra, the single tube from the bladder

295
00:20:08,647 --> 00:20:12,161
out of the body. {3}Two
ureters, one urethra.

296
00:20:15,211 --> 00:20:19,778
And that's the big picture of anatomy
and physiology, from molecules in

297
00:20:19,828 --> 00:20:23,543
one muscle to Jordan
crossing the finish line.

298
00:20:23,593 --> 00:20:27,361
{1}For more practice, there are
free questions on levlprep.com.

299
00:20:27,411 --> 00:20:32,747
{2}Next up: the language of A&P, the
word parts and directional terms that

300
00:20:32,797 --> 00:20:36,143
make every name in this course make sense.

301
00:20:36,193 --> 00:20:37,013
{3}See you there.
